1
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:11,780
When the Quaker Elizabeth married Joseph Fry, it seemed as if her life would be comfortable

2
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:12,880
and peaceful.

3
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:18,160
However, Elizabeth was not content with her easy life and her growing family.

4
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:23,740
She saw many poor people living near her and she wanted to help them.

5
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:28,280
One day she was asked to visit a prison.

6
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:33,380
At first the prison officers did not want to let her visit the women prisoners

7
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:36,320
because they feared the prisoners would attack her,

8
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:38,340
but Elizabeth was not afraid.

9
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:44,280
She realized that the prisoners behaved badly because they were being treated like animals.

10
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:47,660
They had no beds, clean clothes,

11
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:49,020
food or heating.

12
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:54,280
Any child born in prison had to stay there and had no chance of an education.

13
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:59,240
This meant they would probably have to beg or steal when they grew up

14
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:00,880
and then would return to prison.

15
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:05,340
So the first thing Elizabeth did was to provide food,

16
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:07,760
clean clothes and straw for beds.

17
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:13,460
Later she began a prison school for the children and taught the women to sew(缝纫),

18
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:15,460
knit(编织) and make goods to sell.

19
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:19,780
In this way they were able to make a little money for themselves

20
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:21,260
and gain some self-respect.

21
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:25,740
Her kindness helped her gain the friendship of the prisoners

22
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:29,800
and they began to try to improve their conditions for themselves.

23
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:36,760
Later Elizabeth was asked to go to the leaders of Britain to discuss how to improve the conditions

24
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:37,540
for prisoners.

25
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:41,840
Of course she did not do all the work on her own.

26
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,880
Other Quaker women helped her and went around the country raising money

27
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:46,680
for her work.

28
00:01:51,180 --> 00:01:54,700
They said that she should spend more time with her family.

29
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:58,780
Other people said she enjoyed being famous too much.

30
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:03,860
However, her husband, Joseph, supported and encouraged her,

31
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:09,280
so she continued working to help improve the lives of poor prisoners till she died.

32
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:16,300
Her ideas did not disappear after her death and her work was remembered in 1947

33
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:19,320
when the Quakers were given the Nobel Peace Prize.
